    title: 'Ordinary Language and Philosophical Method',
    passageText: `Ordinary language philosophy, dominant in mid-twentieth century Britain, held that most philosophical problems arise from the misuse or misunderstanding of everyday language. Gilbert Ryle identified what he called "category mistakes" — errors that arise when concepts from one logical type are applied to entities of a different type. His famous example was treating the mind as a "ghost in the machine": wrongly applying physical-object language to mental processes, as if the mind were a hidden physical entity inside the body. For Ryle, the traditional mind-body problem was not a deep metaphysical puzzle but a conceptual confusion generated by grammatical misleading.

Ludwig Wittgenstein's later philosophy pursued a related but distinct diagnosis. Where Ryle treated philosophical confusion as category error, Wittgenstein emphasized how language acquires meaning through use in social practices — what he called "language games." A word does not name a private inner object; it derives its meaning from how the community employs it in shared activities. The apparent depth of philosophical problems — questions about the nature of time, of certainty, of the self — reflects, for Wittgenstein, the philosopher being held captive by a picture: a misleading surface grammar that generates pseudo-problems. Philosophical therapy, not philosophical theorizing, is the proper response.

Critics charged both thinkers with a kind of philosophical conservatism: privileging ordinary usage over theoretical innovation, and foreclosing genuine metaphysical inquiry. P.F. Strawson defended a version of "descriptive metaphysics" — mapping the conceptual structure actually embedded in our thought and language — while conceding that "revisionary metaphysics," which seeks to construct better conceptual schemes, remained a legitimate enterprise.`,
